# The circuit, in a light aeroplane: take off, fly the pattern left-hand at
# circuit height, and land back on the runway you left.
#
#   name TEXT...                   what the lesson is called
#   teaches CLASS                  which class of aircraft it is for
#   stage TEXT...                  a stage, in the order flown
#   do TEXT...                     what to do at this stage
#   until PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE     what ends the stage
#   hold PROPERTY LOW HIGH TEXT... a band that must hold for the whole stage
#   need PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE TEXT...  what must be true by the stage's end
#
# with `#` beginning a comment; src/sim/lesson.hpp owns the format.
#
# **The circuit is the first lesson that is a whole flight**, and the only one
# that needs the aeroplane to be somewhere as well as doing something. The
# other five are flown wherever the aeroplane happens to be; this one begins
# and ends on the same runway.
#
# **Two of the stages end on the runner's own properties**, because nothing
# about the aeroplane's state can end them. `lesson/turned-deg` ends a turn -
# a heading cannot, because a circuit turns through three hundred and sixty
# degrees and the wrap at north would end a stage that had not happened.
# `lesson/flown-nm` says the downwind leg was long enough to be one: the leg is
# level and straight, so height, speed and heading read the same at both ends
# of it and how far along it is is the only thing that says where she is.
#
# **A stage that ends on a turn begins part-way through the one before.** The
# turn on to downwind ends its own stage eighty degrees in, and the aeroplane
# is still turning: the last twenty degrees fall inside the downwind leg. So
# the downwind leg ends at fifty degrees of turn, not twenty. At twenty the
# Cub's downwind leg lasted four seconds - the residual of the turn before it
# was nineteen degrees - and the Cessna passed by a single degree, which is
# not passing, it is luck. The turn on to final is measured the same way and
# from the same kind of start, so it asks for a hundred and ten degrees of the
# hundred and thirty that are left rather than the hundred and eighty the turn
# really is.
#
# **The downwind leg ends when she turns, not after a set distance.** A first
# draft ended it after a mile and a half, which is not where the leg ends -
# she goes on downwind until she is far enough out to turn base - so three
# miles of straight and level flying fell inside a stage called "base, and the
# turn on to final", and a sink along the downwind leg was judged by a stage
# that watches no height. It went unremarked.
#
# **The bands are measured, not guessed.** Flown by the AI pilot, the four
# light aircraft hold the climbing speed between 6 knots under it and 9 over,
# sit within 39 feet of circuit height down the whole downwind leg, and cross
# the threshold between 5 knots under the reference speed and 5 over. The
# bands here are those figures with room: a band set by eye is either so wide
# it catches nothing or so narrow it faults correct flying, and both have
# happened in this project.
#
# **The height band is measured from where the leg began, so it catches a
# drift and not an altimeter setting.** An aeroplane flown round the whole
# circuit a hundred feet low is flying a low circuit, which is a different
# fault; one that sinks a hundred feet along the downwind leg is not holding
# her height, which is this one.
#
# **The turns are left**, which is the usual direction and the one the numbers
# here are signed for: `lesson/turned-deg` is negative to the left.
#
# **The circuit begins with a take-off**, because a circuit does. The first
# stage watches no speed: she starts it standing still, and a band on the
# climbing speed would fault every take-off there is for not already being at
# it. The climb out is judged from two hundred feet, by which point the speed
# has settled into the climb.
#
# **Landing is asked of the wheels, not of the altimeter.** `position/h-agl-ft`
# is measured to the aeroplane's own reference point, not to its tyres, and a
# Mosquito parked on the runway reads seven feet - so a lesson that asked for
# five could never be satisfied by one, however well it was landed. `gear/wow`
# is the weight on the wheels, which is the thing being asked about.
#
# **The heights are against where the stage began**, so the lesson can be flown
# from any aerodrome: `start+400` is four hundred feet above where the climb
# out started, not above the sea.

name The circuit in a light aeroplane
teaches light-aircraft

stage The take-off
do Full power, keep her straight on the centreline, and ease her off at the rotation speed
do Let the speed build to the climbing speed once she is off the ground
until position/h-agl-ft >= start+200

stage The climb out
do Climb away at the climbing speed, straight ahead
do Do not turn until you are high enough: five hundred feet is the usual
hold velocities/vc-kts climb-12 climb+15 Hold the climbing speed in the climb out
hold lesson/turned-deg -25 25 Keep her straight until you are high enough to turn
until position/h-agl-ft >= start+400

stage The crosswind turn
do Turn left on to crosswind, still climbing
do A medium turn: this is a climbing turn, not a steep one
until lesson/turned-deg <= -80

stage Crosswind, and the turn on to downwind
do Climb on to circuit height and level off there
do Turn left on to downwind, parallel to the runway and the other way
until lesson/turned-deg <= -80

stage Downwind
do Fly the downwind leg at circuit height, parallel to the runway
do Hold the height and the speed: the landing checks are done along here
hold position/h-agl-ft start-120 start+120 Hold the circuit height downwind
need lesson/flown-nm >= 1.0 Fly a downwind leg long enough to do the checks and settle
until lesson/turned-deg <= -50

stage Base, and the turn on to final
do Power back, turn left on to base and start down
do Turn left on to final and line up with the runway
until lesson/turned-deg <= -110

stage Final
do Hold the approach speed down to the threshold
hold velocities/vc-kts vref-10 vref+15 Hold the approach speed on final
until position/h-agl-ft <= 40

stage The landing
do Close the throttle over the threshold and hold her off
do Let her settle on the main wheels, and keep her straight on the roll
need gear/wow >= 0.5 Land her on the runway
until velocities/vg-fps <= 1.0
